1
00:00:05,519 --> 00:00:10,000
In vain should I attempt to describe the astonishment and disquiet of Herbert,

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,480
when he and I and Provis sat down before the fire, and I recounted the whole of the secret. Enough,

3
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:21,639
that I saw my own feelings reflected in Herbert’s face, and not least among them,

4
00:00:21,641 --> 00:00:25,758
my repugnance towards the man who had done so much for me.

5
00:00:25,758 --> 00:00:30,641
What would alone have set a division between that man and us, if there had been no other

6
00:00:30,641 --> 00:00:37,438
dividing circumstance, was his triumph in my story. Saving his troublesome sense of having

7
00:00:37,438 --> 00:00:43,438
been “low” on one occasion since his return,—on which point he began to hold forth to Herbert,

8
00:00:43,438 --> 00:00:48,800
the moment my revelation was finished,—he had no perception of the possibility of

9
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:54,519
my finding any fault with my good fortune. His boast that he had made me a gentleman,

10
00:00:54,519 --> 00:00:58,957
and that he had come to see me support the character on his ample resources, was made

11
00:00:58,957 --> 00:01:04,358
for me quite as much as for himself. And that it was a highly agreeable boast to both of us,

12
00:01:04,358 --> 00:01:10,917
and that we must both be very proud of it, was a conclusion quite established in his own mind.

13
00:01:10,917 --> 00:01:16,917
“Though, look’ee here, Pip’s comrade,” he said to Herbert, after having discoursed for some time,

14
00:01:16,917 --> 00:01:22,839
“I know very well that once since I come back—for half a minute—I’ve been low. I said to Pip,

15
00:01:22,839 --> 00:01:26,597
I knowed as I had been low. But don’t you fret yourself on that

16
00:01:26,597 --> 00:01:31,000
score. I ain’t made Pip a gentleman, and Pip ain’t a-going to make you a gentleman,

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,758
not fur me not to know what’s due to ye both. Dear boy, and Pip’s comrade,

18
00:01:36,758 --> 00:01:42,957
you two may count upon me always having a genteel muzzle on. Muzzled I have been since that half a

19
00:01:42,957 --> 00:01:50,800
minute when I was betrayed into lowness, muzzled I am at the present time, muzzled I ever will be.”

20
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:57,358
Herbert said, “Certainly,” but looked as if there were no specific consolation in this, and remained

21
00:01:57,358 --> 00:02:03,116
perplexed and dismayed. We were anxious for the time when he would go to his lodging and leave us

22
00:02:03,116 --> 00:02:10,680
together, but he was evidently jealous of leaving us together, and sat late. It was midnight before

23
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:17,358
I took him round to Essex Street, and saw him safely in at his own dark door. When it

24
00:02:17,358 --> 00:02:23,000
closed upon him, I experienced the first moment of relief I had known since the night of his arrival.

25
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,358
Never quite free from an uneasy remembrance of the man on the stairs, I had always looked about me in

26
00:02:28,358 --> 00:02:35,800
taking my guest out after dark, and in bringing him back; and I looked about me now. Difficult

27
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:41,277
as it is in a large city to avoid the suspicion of being watched, when the mind is conscious of

28
00:02:41,277 --> 00:02:47,358
danger in that regard, I could not persuade myself that any of the people within sight cared about

29
00:02:47,358 --> 00:02:54,160
my movements. The few who were passing passed on their several ways, and the street was empty when

30
00:02:54,160 --> 00:03:00,160
I turned back into the Temple. Nobody had come out at the gate with us, nobody went in at the

31
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:07,597
gate with me. As I crossed by the fountain, I saw his lighted back windows looking bright and quiet,

32
00:03:07,597 --> 00:03:11,680
and, when I stood for a few moments in the doorway of the building where I lived, before

33
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:18,641
going up the stairs, Garden Court was as still and lifeless as the staircase was when I ascended it.

34
00:03:18,641 --> 00:03:20,641
Herbert received me with open arms,

35
00:03:21,398 --> 00:03:27,000
and I had never felt before so blessedly what it is to have a friend. When he had spoken some sound

36
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,718
words of sympathy and encouragement, we sat down to consider the question, What was to be done?

37
00:03:33,718 --> 00:03:39,438
The chair that Provis had occupied still remaining where it had stood,—for he had a barrack way with

38
00:03:39,438 --> 00:03:45,199
him of hanging about one spot, in one unsettled manner, and going through one round of observances

39
00:03:45,199 --> 00:03:50,758
with his pipe and his coarse-cut tobacco and his jackknife and his pack of cards, and what not, as

40
00:03:50,758 --> 00:03:56,680
if it were all put down for him on a slate,—I say his chair remaining where it had stood, Herbert

41
00:03:56,680 --> 00:04:03,879
unconsciously took it, but next moment started out of it, pushed it away, and took another.

42
00:04:03,879 --> 00:04:09,800
He had no occasion to say after that that he had conceived an aversion for my patron, neither had

43
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:16,680
I occasion to confess my own. We interchanged that confidence without shaping a syllable.

44
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:22,237
“What,” said I to Herbert, when he was safe in another chair,—“what is to be done?”

45
00:04:22,237 --> 00:04:28,839
“My poor dear Handel,” he replied, holding his head, “I am too stunned to think.”

46
00:04:28,839 --> 00:04:33,277
“So was I, Herbert, when the blow first fell. Still,

47
00:04:33,277 --> 00:04:39,000
something must be done. He is intent upon various new expenses,—horses,

48
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,237
and carriages, and lavish appearances of all kinds. He must be stopped somehow.”

49
00:04:45,237 --> 00:04:47,199
“You mean that you can’t accept—”

50
00:04:47,199 --> 00:04:54,597
“How can I?” I interposed, as Herbert paused. “Think of him! Look at him!”

51
00:04:54,597 --> 00:04:57,917
An involuntary shudder passed over both of us.

52
00:04:57,917 --> 00:04:59,800
“Yet I am afraid the dreadful truth is,

53
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:07,078
Herbert, that he is attached to me, strongly attached to me. Was there ever such a fate!”

54
00:05:07,078 --> 00:05:10,641
“My poor dear Handel,” Herbert repeated.

55
00:05:10,641 --> 00:05:16,879
“Then,” said I, “after all, stopping short here, never taking another penny from him,

56
00:05:16,879 --> 00:05:22,758
think what I owe him already! Then again: I am heavily in debt,—very heavily for me,

57
00:05:22,758 --> 00:05:28,718
who have now no expectations,—and I have been bred to no calling, and I am fit for nothing.”

58
00:05:28,718 --> 00:05:34,116
“Well, well, well!” Herbert remonstrated. “Don’t say fit for nothing.”

59
00:05:34,116 --> 00:05:38,480
“What am I fit for? I know only one thing that I am fit for, and that is,

60
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:43,597
to go for a soldier. And I might have gone, my dear Herbert, but for the prospect of taking

61
00:05:43,597 --> 00:05:49,559
counsel with your friendship and affection.” Of course I broke down there: and of course

62
00:05:49,559 --> 00:05:55,358
Herbert, beyond seizing a warm grip of my hand, pretended not to know it.

63
00:05:55,358 --> 00:06:02,597
“Anyhow, my dear Handel,” said he presently, “soldiering won’t do. If you were to renounce

64
00:06:02,597 --> 00:06:07,680
this patronage and these favours, I suppose you would do so with some faint hope of one

65
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:11,839
day repaying what you have already had. Not very strong, that hope,

66
00:06:11,839 --> 00:06:18,199
if you went soldiering! Besides, it’s absurd. You would be infinitely better in Clarriker’s house,

67
00:06:18,199 --> 00:06:23,160
small as it is. I am working up towards a partnership, you know.”

68
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:26,680
Poor fellow! He little suspected with whose money.

69
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:30,839
“But there is another question,” said Herbert. “This is an ignorant,

70
00:06:30,839 --> 00:06:36,559
determined man, who has long had one fixed idea. More than that,

71
00:06:36,559 --> 00:06:43,000
he seems to me (I may misjudge him) to be a man of a desperate and fierce character.”

72
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:49,116
“I know he is,” I returned. “Let me tell you what evidence I have seen of it.” And I told

73
00:06:49,116 --> 00:06:54,237
him what I had not mentioned in my narrative, of that encounter with the other convict.

74
00:06:54,237 --> 00:06:59,758
“See, then,” said Herbert; “think of this! He comes here at the peril of his life,

75
00:06:59,758 --> 00:07:04,879
for the realisation of his fixed idea. In the moment of realisation,

76
00:07:04,879 --> 00:07:11,398
after all his toil and waiting, you cut the ground from under his feet, destroy his idea, and make

77
00:07:11,398 --> 00:07:18,718
his gains worthless to him. Do you see nothing that he might do, under the disappointment?”

78
00:07:18,718 --> 00:07:24,277
“I have seen it, Herbert, and dreamed of it, ever since the fatal night of his arrival.

79
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:30,319
Nothing has been in my thoughts so distinctly as his putting himself in the way of being taken.”

80
00:07:30,319 --> 00:07:33,398
“Then you may rely upon it,” said Herbert, “that there would be great

81
00:07:33,398 --> 00:07:38,319
danger of his doing it. That is his power over you as long as he remains in England,

82
00:07:38,319 --> 00:07:42,519
and that would be his reckless course if you forsook him.”

83
00:07:42,519 --> 00:07:47,358
I was so struck by the horror of this idea, which had weighed upon me from the first,

84
00:07:47,358 --> 00:07:50,436
and the working out of which would make me regard myself,

85
00:07:50,438 --> 00:07:56,680
in some sort, as his murderer, that I could not rest in my chair, but began pacing to

86
00:07:56,680 --> 00:08:03,758
and fro. I said to Herbert, meanwhile, that even if Provis were recognised and taken,

87
00:08:03,758 --> 00:08:11,839
in spite of himself, I should be wretched as the cause, however innocently. Yes;

88
00:08:11,839 --> 00:08:16,680
even though I was so wretched in having him at large and near me, and even though I would

89
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:22,277
far rather have worked at the forge all the days of my life than I would ever have come to this!

90
00:08:22,277 --> 00:08:26,879
But there was no staving off the question, What was to be done?

91
00:08:26,879 --> 00:08:29,758
“The first and the main thing to be done,” said Herbert,

92
00:08:29,758 --> 00:08:35,237
“is to get him out of England. You will have to go with him, and then he may be induced to go.”

93
00:08:35,237 --> 00:08:39,480
“But get him where I will, could I prevent his coming back?”

94
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:44,639
“My good Handel, is it not obvious that with Newgate in the next street, there must be far

95
00:08:44,641 --> 00:08:50,597
greater hazard in your breaking your mind to him and making him reckless, here, than elsewhere?

96
00:08:50,597 --> 00:08:53,879
If a pretext to get him away could be made out of that other convict,

97
00:08:53,879 --> 00:08:57,758
or out of anything else in his life, now.”

98
00:08:57,758 --> 00:09:02,839
“There, again!” said I, stopping before Herbert, with my open hands held out,

99
00:09:02,839 --> 00:09:08,398
as if they contained the desperation of the case. “I know nothing of his life. It has almost made

100
00:09:08,398 --> 00:09:14,199
me mad to sit here of a night and see him before me, so bound up with my fortunes and misfortunes,

101
00:09:14,199 --> 00:09:20,358
and yet so unknown to me, except as the miserable wretch who terrified me two days in my childhood!”

102
00:09:20,358 --> 00:09:22,639
Herbert got up, and linked his arm in mine,

103
00:09:22,641 --> 00:09:27,199
and we slowly walked to and fro together, studying the carpet.

104
00:09:27,199 --> 00:09:29,639
“Handel,” said Herbert, stopping,

105
00:09:29,641 --> 00:09:33,800
“you feel convinced that you can take no further benefits from him; do you?”

106
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:38,116
“Fully. Surely you would, too, if you were in my place?”

107
00:09:38,116 --> 00:09:41,277
“And you feel convinced that you must break with him?”

108
00:09:41,277 --> 00:09:44,277
“Herbert, can you ask me?”

109
00:09:44,277 --> 00:09:48,839
“And you have, and are bound to have, that tenderness for the life he has risked on

110
00:09:48,839 --> 00:09:54,438
your account, that you must save him, if possible, from throwing it away. Then you

111
00:09:54,438 --> 00:09:59,480
must get him out of England before you stir a finger to extricate yourself. That done,

112
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:04,237
extricate yourself, in Heaven’s name, and we’ll see it out together, dear old boy.”

113
00:10:04,237 --> 00:10:10,358
It was a comfort to shake hands upon it, and walk up and down again, with only that done.

114
00:10:10,358 --> 00:10:16,160
“Now, Herbert,” said I, “with reference to gaining some knowledge of his history.

115
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:20,160
There is but one way that I know of. I must ask him point blank.”

116
00:10:21,438 --> 00:10:27,879
“Yes. Ask him,” said Herbert, “when we sit at breakfast in the morning.” For he had said,

117
00:10:27,879 --> 00:10:32,879
on taking leave of Herbert, that he would come to breakfast with us.

118
00:10:32,879 --> 00:10:38,879
With this project formed, we went to bed. I had the wildest dreams concerning him,

119
00:10:38,879 --> 00:10:44,718
and woke unrefreshed; I woke, too, to recover the fear which I had lost in the night,

120
00:10:44,718 --> 00:10:50,116
of his being found out as a returned transport. Waking, I never lost that fear.

121
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:57,398
He came round at the appointed time, took out his jackknife, and sat down to his meal. He was

122
00:10:57,398 --> 00:11:02,957
full of plans “for his gentleman’s coming out strong, and like a gentleman,” and urged me to

123
00:11:02,957 --> 00:11:08,758
begin speedily upon the pocket-book which he had left in my possession. He considered the chambers

124
00:11:08,758 --> 00:11:14,160
and his own lodging as temporary residences, and advised me to look out at once for a “fashionable

125
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:20,758
crib” near Hyde Park, in which he could have “a shake-down.” When he had made an end of

126
00:11:20,758 --> 00:11:27,559
his breakfast, and was wiping his knife on his leg, I said to him, without a word of preface,—

127
00:11:27,559 --> 00:11:31,358
“After you were gone last night, I told my friend of the struggle that

128
00:11:31,358 --> 00:11:36,358
the soldiers found you engaged in on the marshes, when we came up. You remember?”

129
00:11:36,358 --> 00:11:40,000
“Remember!” said he. “I think so!”

130
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,559
“We want to know something about that man—and about you. It is strange to

131
00:11:44,559 --> 00:11:46,597
know no more about either, and particularly you,

132
00:11:46,597 --> 00:11:52,641
than I was able to tell last night. Is not this as good a time as another for our knowing more?”

133
00:11:52,641 --> 00:11:58,758
“Well!” he said, after consideration. “You’re on your oath, you know, Pip’s comrade?”

134
00:11:58,758 --> 00:12:01,237
“Assuredly,” replied Herbert.

135
00:12:01,237 --> 00:12:06,917
“As to anything I say, you know,” he insisted. “The oath applies to all.”

136
00:12:06,917 --> 00:12:08,839
“I understand it to do so.”

137
00:12:08,839 --> 00:12:13,800
“And look’ee here! Wotever I done is worked out and paid for,” he insisted again.

138
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:15,597
“So be it.”

139
00:12:15,597 --> 00:12:20,116
He took out his black pipe and was going to fill it with coarse-cut tobacco, when,

140
00:12:20,116 --> 00:12:24,758
looking at the tangle of tobacco in his hand, he seemed to think it might perplex the thread

141
00:12:24,758 --> 00:12:30,559
of his narrative. He put it back again, stuck his pipe in a button-hole of his coat,

142
00:12:30,559 --> 00:12:36,641
spread a hand on each knee, and after turning an angry eye on the fire for a few silent moments,

143
00:12:36,641 --> 00:12:40,000
looked round at us and said what follows. Chapter 42.
